\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Sample extraction from \textit {boris} (gunny bags) at the factory site for master sample assessment.}}{11}{figure.caption.26}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Grain sample imaged under the dual-illumination system of Prototype~I. The LCD backlight provides a programmable chromatic substrate beneath the glass tray, while RGB LEDs deliver controlled top-down illumination. The combination renders subtle surface distinctions---between healthy straw-coloured kernels and darkened or discoloured specimens---with sufficient contrast for downstream classification.}}{22}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Prototype~I: the low-fidelity 3D-printed enclosure housing a small-format LCD screen beneath a glass grain surface, with an RGB LED array for top-down illumination. The Raspberry Pi~5 compute platform is visible at the rear. This prototype validated the dual-illumination hypothesis at small scale but could not accommodate the 100-gram industrial sample volume.}}{24}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Prototype~II at industrial scale. (a)~The aluminium chassis provided the structural rigidity and dust resistance required for field deployment, replacing the 3D-printed enclosure. (b)~The density problem in practice: a representative 100-gram sample ($\sim $2,800 entities) demonstrating the multi-layer clustering that motivated the pivot to mechanical dispersion.}}{26}{figure.caption.42}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Exploded view of the finalised Drishti device CAD assembly. The vertical stacking arrangement shows, from bottom to top: the aluminium base housing with solenoid mounting points, the LCD backlight module, the glass imaging surface, the grain tray with precision-milled hole-fitting apertures aligned to the four solenoid plunger positions, and the WS2812B LED ring array for top-down multispectral illumination. The camera module is mounted above the grain plane.}}{35}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Progressive stages of the high-fidelity prototype fabrication process. (a)~The aluminium extrusion frame is cut to specification and assembled to form the structural chassis. (b)~Electromagnetic solenoids are precision-mounted at the four cardinal axis midpoints, aligned with the hole-fitting apertures in the grain tray. (c)~Final wiring integrates the solenoid driver circuits, WS2812B LED array, camera module, and Raspberry Pi~5 compute platform into the enclosed assembly.}}{36}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces The custom PCB designed for the Drishti system, integrating the solenoid driver circuits, LED array controller, and power regulation into a single board. This consolidation reduced the internal wiring complexity from the breadboard-based prototype and improved the reliability of the staggered activation timing critical to the asynchronous pulse logic.}}{36}{figure.caption.54}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces The finalised Drishti device with the hole-fitting grain tray in place. The vertical drop-in interaction requires no lateral motion or retention mechanism: the operator lifts the tray, loads the 100-gram sample, and drops the tray back onto the four solenoid plungers. The hole-fitting apertures in the tray base align automatically with the plunger positions, ensuring direct metal-to-metal contact for zero-damping kinetic energy transfer.}}{47}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces The Drishti digital dashboard: guided workflow interface. (a)~The home screen presents a minimal, single-action entry point. (b)~The guided assessment flow prompts each operator action in sequence, eliminating the need for memorised procedures. (c)~The system communicates real-time progress through each stage of the actuation and imaging cycle, with a visual timeline indicating completed and pending steps.}}{49}{figure.caption.67}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Continuation of the guided workflow. (a)~During image capture, the system takes samples under different illumination settings, providing real-time feedback on the camera's aperture activity. (b)~Upon completion of the inference pipeline, the system generates a comprehensive quality report. Each step in the left-hand timeline is marked with a completion indicator, providing the operator with continuous awareness of the assessment's progress.}}{51}{figure.caption.69}%
